# ti_tm4c.resc -- Renode platform + firmware load for the TM4C1294-style
# Cortex-M4F vendor platform in the holoscan_mcu_lite multi-vendor harness.
#
# WHAT THIS IS
#   A 1 MiB flash mapped at address 0, 256 KiB of SRAM, one console UART and the
#   LoadELF of this board's demo image. Deliberately NO network peripheral and NO
#   SDRAM window: this smoke test sends and receives nothing, and this part has
#   no external SDRAM in this platform, so a plausible-looking Ethernet MAC or SDRAM
#   window here would be an invitation to claim coverage and memory that do not
#   exist.
#
# MEMORY MAP (kept in sync by renode/scripts/selfcheck_vendors.sh against
# firmware/platform/gcc_ti_tm4c_hml_mcu.ld, firmware/platform/board_config.h and
# the ti_tm4c row of renode/boards/index -- all four must agree or the
# ti_tm4c_renode_selfcheck CTest test fails)
#   0x00000000  1 MiB   FLASH   code, rodata, .data initialiser image
#   0x20000000  256 KiB SRAM    .data, .bss, stack, newlib heap
#   0x4000C000          UART0    the only peripheral (data +0x00, flags +0x18)
#
# THIS IS THE ONLY ROW IN THE MATRIX WITH FLASH AT ADDRESS ZERO
#   The TM4C1294 boots from 0, so the reset vector table is at 0 and the
#   VectorTableOffset below is spelled out literally. That matters more here than
#   on the other rows: an image entered at the wrong address would land in code
#   rather than in obviously empty space, which is the quietest way to fail.
#   selfcheck_vendors.sh asserts that line equals the FLASH ORIGIN in the linker
#   script.
#
# ABOUT THE FPU
#   cpuType is "cortex-m4f" because that is what the part is and the platform
#   model is allowed to have an FPU. The firmware is compiled -mfloat-abi=soft
#   and contains no FP instruction: nothing in this harness needs one, and
#   enabling CPACR is startup-file work. An FPU in the model that the image never
#   touches is the intended state.
#
# ABOUT THE UART MODEL NAME
#   The TM4C UART is a TI Stellaris PL411. There is no TM4C UART model that this
#   harness has ever seen and Renode is not installed anywhere this matrix was
#   written, so this row asks for UART.PL011, whose register block (data at +0x00,
#   flags at +0x18, TXFF as bit 5) is the closest match available, and
#   firmware/platform/startup_ti_tm4c.c writes that block to match. The two halves
#   agree, which is what lets the image print at all; they have NOT been verified
#   against a data sheet or a Renode release. If a real TM4C model turns up,
#   change both halves.
#
# WHY THE HEAP IS IN SRAM AND SMALL
#   hml_reasm_open(&r, 0) uses the library default frame size of 1 MiB and
#   mallocs TWO buffers, i.e. 2 MiB of heap, which is why the BASE platform
#   (renode/holoscan_mcu_lite.resc) needs its 4 MiB SDRAM window. This board is
#   built with --frame-bytes 65536: 138 KiB of heap inside 256 KiB of SRAM, after
#   an 8 KiB stack. Same code paths, same checks, smaller frame cap.
#
# STATUS -- READ BEFORE TRUSTING THIS FILE
#   This platform description HAS NEVER BEEN EXECUTED. The machine this matrix
#   was written on has no Renode, no mono, no dotnet, no arm-none-eabi-gcc, and
#   github.com and downloads.renode.io are unreachable from it. Nothing here has
#   been loaded by a Renode release, and no TM4C1294 data sheet was available to
#   check the interrupt line (5) against.
#   That is exactly why the CTest test that uses it exits 77 (skipped) when the
#   tooling is missing. If the tooling IS present and the platform does not load,
#   run_vendor_smoke.sh returns the emulator's non-zero exit and the test FAILS --
#   loudly, and never as a pass that was not observed.

:name: ti_tm4c
:description: ti_tm4c vendor platform (TM4C1294-style Cortex-M4F, flash @ 0x00000000, UART0 @ 0x4000C000, 1 MiB flash, 256 KiB SRAM, no SDRAM). Emulator only; never executed; not silicon.

$elf?=ti_tm4c_hml_mcu_demo.elf

mach create "hml_ti_tm4c"
machine LoadPlatformDescriptionFromString
"""
cpu: CPU.CortexM @ sysbus
    cpuType: "cortex-m4f"
    nvic: nvic

nvic: IRQControllers.NVIC @ sysbus 0xE000E000
    -> cpu@0

flash: Memory.MappedMemory @ sysbus 0x00000000
    size: 0x100000

sram: Memory.MappedMemory @ sysbus 0x20000000
    size: 0x40000

uart: UART.PL011 @ sysbus 0x4000C000
    -> nvic@5
"""

sysbus LoadELF $elf
# The Cortex-M takes its initial SP and reset vector from the first two words of
# the region LoadELF placed the image in. On this part that is address 0, so the
# offset is the literal 0x00000000 -- written out rather than left as 0 so that
# selfcheck_vendors.sh has something specific to assert against the linker
# script's FLASH ORIGIN.
cpu VectorTableOffset 0x00000000

# No showAnalyzer: under renode-test --console the UART is captured by the robot
# file's Create Terminal Tester, and an interactive analyzer window would only
# add a way for the run to fail for a non-code reason.
